1
00:00:05,798 --> 00:00:11,000
Bentley Drummle, who was so sulky a fellow that he even took up a book as if its writer had done him

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,919
an injury, did not take up an acquaintance in a more agreeable spirit. Heavy in figure, movement,

3
00:00:17,919 --> 00:00:23,359
and comprehension,—in the sluggish complexion of his face, and in the large, awkward tongue

4
00:00:23,359 --> 00:00:29,398
that seemed to loll about in his mouth as he himself lolled about in a room,—he was idle,

5
00:00:29,398 --> 00:00:37,438
proud, niggardly, reserved, and suspicious. He came of rich people down in Somersetshire,

6
00:00:37,438 --> 00:00:41,797
who had nursed this combination of qualities until they made the discovery that it was

7
00:00:41,798 --> 00:00:48,039
just of age and a blockhead. Thus, Bentley Drummle had come to Mr. Pocket when he was

8
00:00:48,039 --> 00:00:54,000
a head taller than that gentleman, and half a dozen heads thicker than most gentlemen.

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,478
Startop had been spoilt by a weak mother and kept at home when he ought to have been at school,

10
00:01:00,478 --> 00:01:06,759
but he was devotedly attached to her, and admired her beyond measure. He had a woman’s delicacy

11
00:01:06,759 --> 00:01:13,240
of feature, and was—“as you may see, though you never saw her,” said Herbert to me—“exactly like

12
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:18,958
his mother.” It was but natural that I should take to him much more kindly than to Drummle,

13
00:01:18,958 --> 00:01:23,638
and that, even in the earliest evenings of our boating, he and I should pull homeward abreast

14
00:01:23,638 --> 00:01:29,240
of one another, conversing from boat to boat, while Bentley Drummle came up in our wake alone,

15
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:34,519
under the overhanging banks and among the rushes. He would always creep in-shore like

16
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:41,638
some uncomfortable amphibious creature, even when the tide would have sent him fast upon his way;

17
00:01:41,638 --> 00:01:48,438
and I always think of him as coming after us in the dark or by the back-water,

18
00:01:48,438 --> 00:01:54,438
when our own two boats were breaking the sunset or the moonlight in mid-stream.

19
00:01:54,438 --> 00:02:01,558
Herbert was my intimate companion and friend. I presented him with a half-share in my boat,

20
00:02:01,559 --> 00:02:06,278
which was the occasion of his often coming down to Hammersmith; and my possession of

21
00:02:06,278 --> 00:02:11,679
a half-share in his chambers often took me up to London. We used to walk between

22
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:16,958
the two places at all hours. I have an affection for the road yet (though it is

23
00:02:16,958 --> 00:02:24,559
not so pleasant a road as it was then), formed in the impressibility of untried youth and hope.

24
00:02:24,559 --> 00:02:30,759
When I had been in Mr. Pocket’s family a month or two, Mr. and Mrs. Camilla turned up. Camilla

25
00:02:30,759 --> 00:02:36,599
was Mr. Pocket’s sister. Georgiana, whom I had seen at Miss Havisham’s on the same occasion,

26
00:02:36,599 --> 00:02:43,879
also turned up. She was a cousin,—an indigestive single woman, who called her rigidity religion,

27
00:02:43,879 --> 00:02:50,759
and her liver love. These people hated me with the hatred of cupidity and disappointment. As a matter

28
00:02:50,759 --> 00:02:59,039
of course, they fawned upon me in my prosperity with the basest meanness. Towards Mr. Pocket,

29
00:02:59,039 --> 00:03:04,317
as a grown-up infant with no notion of his own interests, they showed the complacent forbearance

30
00:03:04,317 --> 00:03:11,159
I had heard them express. Mrs. Pocket they held in contempt; but they allowed the poor soul to have

31
00:03:11,159 --> 00:03:18,080
been heavily disappointed in life, because that shed a feeble reflected light upon themselves.

32
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:21,118
These were the surroundings among which I settled down,

33
00:03:21,118 --> 00:03:26,958
and applied myself to my education. I soon contracted expensive habits, and began to

34
00:03:26,958 --> 00:03:32,840
spend an amount of money that within a few short months I should have thought almost fabulous;

35
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:40,159
but through good and evil I stuck to my books. There was no other merit in this, than my having

36
00:03:40,159 --> 00:03:47,519
sense enough to feel my deficiencies. Between Mr. Pocket and Herbert I got on fast; and,

37
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:52,559
with one or the other always at my elbow to give me the start I wanted, and clear

38
00:03:52,559 --> 00:03:58,520
obstructions out of my road, I must have been as great a dolt as Drummle if I had done less.

39
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:03,199
I had not seen Mr. Wemmick for some weeks, when I thought I would write him a note and propose

40
00:04:03,199 --> 00:04:08,840
to go home with him on a certain evening. He replied that it would give him much pleasure,

41
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:13,759
and that he would expect me at the office at six o’clock. Thither I went,

42
00:04:13,759 --> 00:04:19,240
and there I found him, putting the key of his safe down his back as the clock struck.

43
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:22,558
“Did you think of walking down to Walworth?” said he.

44
00:04:22,559 --> 00:04:25,719
“Certainly,” said I, “if you approve.”

45
00:04:25,719 --> 00:04:30,680
“Very much,” was Wemmick’s reply, “for I have had my legs under the desk all day,

46
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:35,520
and shall be glad to stretch them. Now, I’ll tell you what I have got for supper,

47
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:43,398
Mr. Pip. I have got a stewed steak,—which is of home preparation,—and a cold roast fowl,—which

48
00:04:43,398 --> 00:04:49,519
is from the cook’s-shop. I think it’s tender, because the master of the shop was a Juryman

49
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:55,798
in some cases of ours the other day, and we let him down easy. I reminded him of it when

50
00:04:55,798 --> 00:05:00,719
I bought the fowl, and I said, “Pick us out a good one, old Briton, because if we had chosen

51
00:05:00,719 --> 00:05:06,317
to keep you in the box another day or two, we could easily have done it.” He said to that,

52
00:05:06,317 --> 00:05:12,118
“Let me make you a present of the best fowl in the shop.” I let him, of course. As far as it goes,

53
00:05:12,118 --> 00:05:17,317
it’s property and portable. You don’t object to an aged parent, I hope?”

54
00:05:17,317 --> 00:05:21,558
I really thought he was still speaking of the fowl, until he added, “Because I

55
00:05:21,559 --> 00:05:27,080
have got an aged parent at my place.” I then said what politeness required.

56
00:05:27,080 --> 00:05:31,798
“So, you haven’t dined with Mr. Jaggers yet?” he pursued, as we walked along.

57
00:05:31,798 --> 00:05:34,798
“Not yet.” “He told me so this

58
00:05:34,798 --> 00:05:40,759
afternoon when he heard you were coming. I expect you’ll have an invitation to-morrow. He’s going to

59
00:05:40,759 --> 00:05:46,638
ask your pals, too. Three of ’em; ain’t there?” Although I was not in the habit of counting

60
00:05:46,638 --> 00:05:50,719
Drummle as one of my intimate associates, I answered, “Yes.”

61
00:05:50,719 --> 00:05:55,840
“Well, he’s going to ask the whole gang,”—I hardly felt complimented by the word,—“and whatever he

62
00:05:55,840 --> 00:06:01,638
gives you, he’ll give you good. Don’t look forward to variety, but you’ll have excellence. And

63
00:06:01,638 --> 00:06:07,159
there’s another rum thing in his house,” proceeded Wemmick, after a moment’s pause, as if the remark

64
00:06:07,159 --> 00:06:13,398
followed on the housekeeper understood; “he never lets a door or window be fastened at night.”

65
00:06:13,398 --> 00:06:15,558
“Is he never robbed?”

66
00:06:15,559 --> 00:06:20,000
“That’s it!” returned Wemmick. “He says, and gives it out publicly,

67
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:26,680
“I want to see the man who’ll rob me.” Lord bless you, I have heard him, a hundred times,

68
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:32,398
if I have heard him once, say to regular cracksmen in our front office, “You know where I live;

69
00:06:32,398 --> 00:06:38,000
now, no bolt is ever drawn there; why don’t you do a stroke of business with me? Come;

70
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,840
can’t I tempt you?” Not a man of them, sir, would be bold enough to try it on, for love or money.”

71
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:47,759
“They dread him so much?” said I.

72
00:06:47,759 --> 00:06:53,398
“Dread him,” said Wemmick. “I believe you they dread him. Not but what he’s artful,

73
00:06:53,398 --> 00:07:00,359
even in his defiance of them. No silver, sir. Britannia metal, every spoon.”

74
00:07:00,359 --> 00:07:04,438
“So they wouldn’t have much,” I observed, “even if they—”

75
00:07:04,438 --> 00:07:07,679
“Ah! But he would have much,” said Wemmick, cutting me short,

76
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:12,840
“and they know it. He’d have their lives, and the lives of scores of ’em. He’d have all he

77
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:18,919
could get. And it’s impossible to say what he couldn’t get, if he gave his mind to it.”

78
00:07:18,919 --> 00:07:24,359
I was falling into meditation on my guardian’s greatness, when Wemmick remarked:—

79
00:07:24,359 --> 00:07:30,199
“As to the absence of plate, that’s only his natural depth, you know. A river’s its natural

80
00:07:30,199 --> 00:07:36,558
depth, and he’s his natural depth. Look at his watch-chain. That’s real enough.”

81
00:07:36,559 --> 00:07:39,199
“It’s very massive,” said I.

82
00:07:39,199 --> 00:07:45,616
“Massive?” repeated Wemmick. “I think so. And his watch is a gold repeater, and worth a hundred

83
00:07:45,616 --> 00:07:52,000
pound if it’s worth a penny. Mr. Pip, there are about seven hundred thieves in this town who know

84
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:58,478
all about that watch; there’s not a man, a woman, or a child, among them, who wouldn’t identify the

85
00:07:58,478 --> 00:08:07,118
smallest link in that chain, and drop it as if it was red hot, if inveigled into touching it.”

86
00:08:07,118 --> 00:08:12,680
At first with such discourse, and afterwards with conversation of a more general nature,

87
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:15,278
did Mr. Wemmick and I beguile the time and the

88
00:08:15,278 --> 00:08:20,879
road, until he gave me to understand that we had arrived in the district of Walworth.

89
00:08:20,879 --> 00:08:26,398
It appeared to be a collection of back lanes, ditches, and little gardens, and to present

90
00:08:26,398 --> 00:08:33,159
the aspect of a rather dull retirement. Wemmick’s house was a little wooden cottage in the midst of

91
00:08:33,159 --> 00:08:38,798
plots of garden, and the top of it was cut out and painted like a battery mounted with guns.

92
00:08:38,798 --> 00:08:43,759
“My own doing,” said Wemmick. “Looks pretty; don’t it?”

93
00:08:43,759 --> 00:08:50,798
I highly commended it, I think it was the smallest house I ever saw; with the queerest gothic windows

94
00:08:50,798 --> 00:08:57,278
(by far the greater part of them sham), and a gothic door almost too small to get in at.

95
00:08:57,278 --> 00:09:04,118
“That’s a real flagstaff, you see,” said Wemmick, “and on Sundays I run up a real flag. Then look

96
00:09:04,118 --> 00:09:12,598
here. After I have crossed this bridge, I hoist it up—so—and cut off the communication.”

97
00:09:12,599 --> 00:09:15,039
The bridge was a plank, and it crossed a chasm

98
00:09:15,559 --> 00:09:20,638
about four feet wide and two deep. But it was very pleasant to see the pride with which he

99
00:09:20,638 --> 00:09:28,759
hoisted it up and made it fast; smiling as he did so, with a relish and not merely mechanically.

100
00:09:28,759 --> 00:09:34,278
“At nine o’clock every night, Greenwich time,” said Wemmick, “the gun fires. There he is,

101
00:09:34,278 --> 00:09:39,159
you see! And when you hear him go, I think you’ll say he’s a Stinger.”

102
00:09:39,159 --> 00:09:43,199
The piece of ordnance referred to, was mounted in a separate fortress,

103
00:09:43,199 --> 00:09:46,118
constructed of lattice-work. It was protected from

104
00:09:46,118 --> 00:09:51,798
the weather by an ingenious little tarpaulin contrivance in the nature of an umbrella.

105
00:09:51,798 --> 00:09:56,719
“Then, at the back,” said Wemmick, “out of sight, so as not to impede

106
00:09:56,719 --> 00:10:01,559
the idea of fortifications,—for it’s a principle with me, if you have an idea,

107
00:10:01,559 --> 00:10:05,879
carry it out and keep it up,—I don’t know whether that’s your opinion—”

108
00:10:05,879 --> 00:10:07,599
I said, decidedly.

109
00:10:07,599 --> 00:10:12,199
“—At the back, there’s a pig, and there are fowls and rabbits; then,

110
00:10:12,199 --> 00:10:17,477
I knock together my own little frame, you see, and grow cucumbers; and you’ll judge at supper

111
00:10:17,478 --> 00:10:24,559
what sort of a salad I can raise. So, sir,” said Wemmick, smiling again, but seriously too,

112
00:10:24,559 --> 00:10:29,559
as he shook his head, “if you can suppose the little place besieged, it would hold

113
00:10:29,559 --> 00:10:36,199
out a devil of a time in point of provisions.” Then, he conducted me to a bower about a dozen

114
00:10:36,199 --> 00:10:41,278
yards off, but which was approached by such ingenious twists of path that it took quite

115
00:10:41,278 --> 00:10:48,438
a long time to get at; and in this retreat our glasses were already set forth. Our punch was

116
00:10:48,438 --> 00:10:55,240
cooling in an ornamental lake, on whose margin the bower was raised. This piece of water (with

117
00:10:55,240 --> 00:11:00,759
an island in the middle which might have been the salad for supper) was of a circular form,

118
00:11:00,759 --> 00:11:06,240
and he had constructed a fountain in it, which, when you set a little mill going and took a cork

119
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:12,679
out of a pipe, played to that powerful extent that it made the back of your hand quite wet.

120
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:18,638
“I am my own engineer, and my own carpenter, and my own plumber, and my own gardener,

121
00:11:18,638 --> 00:11:25,080
and my own Jack of all Trades,” said Wemmick, in acknowledging my compliments. “Well;

122
00:11:25,080 --> 00:11:31,317
it’s a good thing, you know. It brushes the Newgate cobwebs away, and pleases the Aged.

123
00:11:31,317 --> 00:11:37,637
You wouldn’t mind being at once introduced to the Aged, would you? It wouldn’t put you out?”

124
00:11:37,638 --> 00:11:43,359
I expressed the readiness I felt, and we went into the castle. There we found,

125
00:11:43,359 --> 00:11:47,919
sitting by a fire, a very old man in a flannel coat: clean,

126
00:11:47,919 --> 00:11:52,958
cheerful, comfortable, and well cared for, but intensely deaf.

127
00:11:52,958 --> 00:11:54,840
“Well aged parent,” said Wemmick,

128
00:11:54,840 --> 00:11:59,240
shaking hands with him in a cordial and jocose way, “how am you?”

129
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:02,637
“All right, John; all right!” replied the old man.

130
00:12:02,638 --> 00:12:08,039
“Here’s Mr. Pip, aged parent,” said Wemmick, “and I wish you could hear his name. Nod away at him,

131
00:12:08,039 --> 00:12:13,359
Mr. Pip; that’s what he likes. Nod away at him, if you please, like winking!”

132
00:12:13,359 --> 00:12:18,958
“This is a fine place of my son’s, sir,” cried the old man, while I nodded as hard as I possibly

133
00:12:18,958 --> 00:12:24,278
could. “This is a pretty pleasure-ground, sir. This spot and these beautiful works

134
00:12:24,278 --> 00:12:30,398
upon it ought to be kept together by the Nation, after my son’s time, for the people’s enjoyment.”

135
00:12:30,398 --> 00:12:35,637
“You’re as proud of it as Punch; ain’t you, Aged?” said Wemmick, contemplating the old man,

136
00:12:35,638 --> 00:12:42,398
with his hard face really softened; “there’s a nod for you;” giving him a tremendous one;

137
00:12:42,398 --> 00:12:46,359
“there’s another for you;” giving him a still more tremendous one;

138
00:12:46,359 --> 00:12:50,718
“you like that, don’t you? If you’re not tired, Mr. Pip—though I know it’s

139
00:12:50,719 --> 00:12:56,559
tiring to strangers—will you tip him one more? You can’t think how it pleases him.”

140
00:12:56,559 --> 00:13:01,159
I tipped him several more, and he was in great spirits. We left him

141
00:13:01,159 --> 00:13:06,638
bestirring himself to feed the fowls, and we sat down to our punch in the arbour;

142
00:13:06,638 --> 00:13:10,599
where Wemmick told me, as he smoked a pipe, that it had taken him a good

143
00:13:10,599 --> 00:13:14,840
many years to bring the property up to its present pitch of perfection.

144
00:13:15,559 --> 00:13:18,080
“Is it your own, Mr. Wemmick?”

145
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:24,240
“O yes,” said Wemmick, “I have got hold of it, a bit at a time. It’s a freehold, by George!”

146
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:28,000
“Is it indeed? I hope Mr. Jaggers admires it?”

147
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:33,759
“Never seen it,” said Wemmick. “Never heard of it. Never seen the Aged. Never heard of him. No;

148
00:13:33,759 --> 00:13:39,080
the office is one thing, and private life is another. When I go into the office,

149
00:13:39,080 --> 00:13:42,680
I leave the Castle behind me, and when I come into the Castle,

150
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:47,278
I leave the office behind me. If it’s not in any way disagreeable to you,

151
00:13:47,278 --> 00:13:53,118
you’ll oblige me by doing the same. I don’t wish it professionally spoken about.”

152
00:13:53,118 --> 00:13:59,159
Of course I felt my good faith involved in the observance of his request. The punch being very

153
00:13:59,159 --> 00:14:05,080
nice, we sat there drinking it and talking, until it was almost nine o’clock. “Getting

154
00:14:05,080 --> 00:14:12,680
near gun-fire,” said Wemmick then, as he laid down his pipe; “it’s the Aged’s treat.”

155
00:14:12,680 --> 00:14:19,080
Proceeding into the Castle again, we found the Aged heating the poker, with expectant eyes,

156
00:14:19,080 --> 00:14:25,879
as a preliminary to the performance of this great nightly ceremony. Wemmick stood with his watch in

157
00:14:25,879 --> 00:14:31,879
his hand until the moment was come for him to take the red-hot poker from the Aged, and repair to the

158
00:14:31,879 --> 00:14:38,478
battery. He took it, and went out, and presently the Stinger went off with a Bang that shook the

159
00:14:38,478 --> 00:14:44,199
crazy little box of a cottage as if it must fall to pieces, and made every glass and teacup in it

160
00:14:44,199 --> 00:14:50,637
ring. Upon this, the Aged—who I believe would have been blown out of his arm-chair but for

161
00:14:50,638 --> 00:14:57,359
holding on by the elbows—cried out exultingly, “He’s fired! I heerd him!” and I nodded at the

162
00:14:57,359 --> 00:15:03,637
old gentleman until it is no figure of speech to declare that I absolutely could not see him.

163
00:15:03,638 --> 00:15:07,000
The interval between that time and supper Wemmick devoted to showing

164
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:12,159
me his collection of curiosities. They were mostly of a felonious character;

165
00:15:12,159 --> 00:15:18,000
comprising the pen with which a celebrated forgery had been committed, a distinguished razor or two,

166
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:23,278
some locks of hair, and several manuscript confessions written under condemnation,—upon

167
00:15:23,278 --> 00:15:29,159
which Mr. Wemmick set particular value as being, to use his own words, “every one of ’em Lies,

168
00:15:29,159 --> 00:15:36,477
sir.” These were agreeably dispersed among small specimens of china and glass, various neat trifles

169
00:15:36,478 --> 00:15:42,638
made by the proprietor of the museum, and some tobacco-stoppers carved by the Aged. They were

170
00:15:42,638 --> 00:15:48,039
all displayed in that chamber of the Castle into which I had been first inducted, and which served,

171
00:15:48,039 --> 00:15:53,638
not only as the general sitting-room but as the kitchen too, if I might judge from a saucepan on

172
00:15:53,638 --> 00:16:00,039
the hob, and a brazen bijou over the fireplace designed for the suspension of a roasting-jack.

173
00:16:00,039 --> 00:16:06,039
There was a neat little girl in attendance, who looked after the Aged in the day. When she had

174
00:16:06,039 --> 00:16:11,240
laid the supper-cloth, the bridge was lowered to give her means of egress, and she withdrew for

175
00:16:11,240 --> 00:16:18,359
the night. The supper was excellent; and though the Castle was rather subject to dry-rot insomuch

176
00:16:18,359 --> 00:16:24,039
that it tasted like a bad nut, and though the pig might have been farther off, I was heartily

177
00:16:24,039 --> 00:16:29,958
pleased with my whole entertainment. Nor was there any drawback on my little turret bedroom,

178
00:16:29,958 --> 00:16:35,080
beyond there being such a very thin ceiling between me and the flagstaff, that when I lay

179
00:16:35,080 --> 00:16:41,599
down on my back in bed, it seemed as if I had to balance that pole on my forehead all night.

180
00:16:41,599 --> 00:16:46,759
Wemmick was up early in the morning, and I am afraid I heard him cleaning my boots. After that,

181
00:16:46,759 --> 00:16:51,840
he fell to gardening, and I saw him from my gothic window pretending to employ the Aged,

182
00:16:51,840 --> 00:16:57,240
and nodding at him in a most devoted manner. Our breakfast was as good as the supper,

183
00:16:57,240 --> 00:17:04,477
and at half-past eight precisely we started for Little Britain. By degrees, Wemmick got dryer

184
00:17:04,478 --> 00:17:11,278
and harder as we went along, and his mouth tightened into a post-office again. At last,

185
00:17:11,278 --> 00:17:15,438
when we got to his place of business and he pulled out his key from his coat-collar,

186
00:17:15,438 --> 00:17:21,080
he looked as unconscious of his Walworth property as if the Castle and the drawbridge and the arbour

187
00:17:21,080 --> 00:17:25,958
and the lake and the fountain and the Aged, had all been blown into space together by the last

